  majority fingerprint: score_hist=((0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 1), (4, 1)) c3=0 scc=(1, 1, 1, 1, 1) hp=1
  majority Hamiltonian order: q > sum > product > numpow > denpow

[Proof readout]
  The ordinary denominator q is not just a scale; it is the binding-scale label.
  The sum payload p+q is the least destructive mutation: on the Farey child chain it
  is exactly linear, and on the row bank it is close to q but still not exact.
  The product payload p*q is the additive/multiplicative bridge: it remembers both
  coordinates and echoes the repo's pair-sum/2n-1 shell arithmetic, but it creates
  more global inversions than sum.
  The power payloads are useful as magnitude amplifiers for unbounded false positives,
  not as local proof denominators: they destroy too much Farey adjacency.
  LRC14 target sharpened by this scout:
    use q/binding-scale for the theorem; use sum/product as side-channel ledgers;
    reserve power variants for detecting unbounded magnitude leakage in fixed-rule
    tournament discriminators such as floor-odd or CF-parity.
